 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-* **Intersecting Models:** If two watertight models intersect, the algorithm assumes the symmetric difference, not the union [S1, unofficial].
-* **DX9 Performance:** Without MRT support, DX9 still extracts 32 layers per pass, but performance converges to ~23x speedup over [5] rather than 4x, possibly due to G6 MRT sweet spot [S1, unofficial].
-* **DX9 Memory:** For DX9, allocating a 32-bit integer lookup texture would require $2^{32}$ entries, which does not fit in GPU memory; breaking integers into 16-bit parts implies 64 lookups per voxel column, making bitwise arithmetic more efficient [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Rasterization Offset:** Standard rasterization causes the voxelization to sample centers of a voxel grid shifted by half a voxel along the z-axis. This can be counteracted by adding half a voxel to the fragment's distance before quantizing [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Intersecting Models:** When two watertight models intersect, the algorithm computes the symmetric difference (XOR), resulting in a hollow representation rather than a union. This is consistent with the definition of watertight based on the Jordan theorem [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Ambiguity:** Ambiguous situations occur when several front and back-facing triangles fall in one voxel. The XOR accumulation handles this parity logic implicitly, making the method accurate even if an arbitrary number of fragments fall in a single voxel [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Clipping:** Polygons must not be clipped by near/far planes. Use Depth Clamp to ensure correct intersection counting [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
-### Conservative Solid Voxelization
-A technique to fill voxels that are partially interior, addressing the issue where thin geometry misses voxel centers and is annihilated by XOR [S1, unofficial]. It renders the scene once with the standard single-pass solid voxelization algorithm, then performs a conservative boundary voxelization [38] and adds it to the same texture [S1, unofficial]. Implementation uses a small 1D bitmask texture to transform depth extremes into bitmasks, shifting the farther depth value by one voxel distance [S1, unofficial]. The XOR of bitmasks activates voxels between extents, blended with an OR operation to create the conservative boundary [S1, unofficial]. To get conservative interior only (no boundary), subtract the conservative boundary voxelization from the conservative overestimate using XOR: $Result = ConservativeInterior \text{ XOR } ConservativeBoundary$ [S1, unofficial].
+### [Single-pass Solid Voxelization via XOR Bitmask Accumulation](3325-single-pass-solid-voxelization-via-xor-bitmask-accumulation.md)
+This method voxelize watertight models in a single GPU render pass by exploiting the Jordan curve theorem: a point is interior if a ray from it intersects the surface an odd number of times. It accumulates fragment bitmasks using bitwise XOR to determine voxel occupancy, distinguishing itself from boundary-only voxelization by producing a fully filled interior volume. It builds on [Single-pass GPU Solid Voxelization](3323-single-pass-gpu-solid-voxelization.md).
+
+### [Conservative Solid Voxelization](3563-conservative-solid-voxelization.md)
+A technique to fill voxels that are partially interior, addressing the issue where thin geometry misses voxel centers and is annihilated by XOR.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** The method achieves significant speedups over previous work [5] [S1, unofficial]. For DX10, speedup is up to >300x for complex models and ~20x for a simple cube (12 triangles) [S1, unofficial]. For DX9, speedup is ~2x without MRT, converging to 23x for models up to 12,500 triangles [S1, unofficial].
-**Memory Complexity:** Memory efficient due to tight packing of information [S1, unofficial]. Uses a small 1D bitmask texture instead of large 2D textures [S1, unofficial].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+*   **Time:** Single geometry pass. Performance depends on fill rate and geometry complexity.
+*   **Memory:** O(W * H) for the slicemap texture, where W, H are the XY dimensions of the voxel grid. The Z dimension is packed into the bits of the texture.
+
 **Measured Performance:**
-* **DX10 (G80) Density Computation:** 0.20 ms at $64^3$, 0.6 ms at $128^3$, 2.9 ms at $256^3$, 20.9 ms at $512^3$ [S1, unofficial].
-* **DX9 Density Computation:** 0.28 ms at $256^3$, 0.9 ms at $512^3$, 6.5 ms at $1024^3$ [S1, unofficial].
-**Strengths:** High speed, accuracy even with arbitrary fragments per voxel, no front/back face separation needed, works with non-manifold watertight meshes [S1, unofficial].
-**Limitations:** DX9 bitwise arithmetic does not scale for DX10 hardware because a 32-bit integer lookup texture would require $2^{32}$ entries, which does not fit in GPU memory [S1, unofficial].
+*   **DX10 (G80):**
+    *   Density computation timings: 0.20 ms at $64^3$, 0.6 ms at $128^3$, 2.9 ms at $256^3$, 20.9 ms at $512^3$ [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Speedup over CPU-based method [5]: >300x for complex models; ~20x for a simple 12-triangle cube (worst-case due to low geometric complexity and high fill-rate) [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Theoretical maximum speedup is 1024x due to multi-layer extraction per pass [S2, unofficial].
+*   **DX9 (G6):**
+    *   Density computation timings: 0.28 ms at $256^3$, 0.9 ms at $512^3$, 6.5 ms at $1024^3$ [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Speedup over [5]: ~23x for models up to 12,500 triangles [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Without MRT support, 32 layers are extracted per pass. Performance is ~2x the cost of 4 MRTs but still yields significant speedup [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   **Strengths:** Single pass; handles arbitrary fragment density per voxel accurately; does not require manifold meshes or front/back face separation; handles grazing angles automatically [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Only works for watertight models; intersecting models result in symmetric difference (hollow) rather than union; standard rasterization framerate drops quickly for high-polygon models and high voxel resolutions [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Hardware Specifics:** DX9 implementation treats all four color components equally, reflecting vector capacities of older processors [S1, unofficial]. Using 4 MRTs imposes more than the expected 33% supplementary workload compared to 3 MRTs, likely due to hardware sweet spots (e.g., G6) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the paper "Single-pass GPU Solid Voxelization and Applications" [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to provide an efficient single-pass solution for solid voxelization, which was previously lacking in efficiency [S1, unofficial]. The method is useful for applications like conservative visibility testing [7] and density/normal estimation [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of solid voxelization [1695-solid-voxelization.md] but optimizes it for GPU architectures, particularly DX10 and DX9 [S1, unofficial].
+**Origin:**
+The method was introduced by Elmar Eisemann and Xavier Decoret in their paper "Single-Pass GPU Solid Voxelization for Real-Time Applications" [S2, unofficial]. It builds upon previous work on GPU boundary voxelization by Dong et al. [8] and Eisemann & Decoret [10] [S2, unofficial].
+
+**History and Development:**
+Prior methods like depth peeling required multiple passes. The stencil buffer approach [5] was limited to one voxel layer per rendering pass. This method leverages the bitwise capabilities of modern GPUs (DX10/DX9) to pack up to 1024 binary layers into a single pass using XOR accumulation [S2, unofficial]. It was demonstrated in applications such as translucency effects, CSG operations, particle simulation interaction, and morphological operations [S2, unofficial].
 
